Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for the Medicare Enrollment Dashboard

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package (v1.4.1) using npm
- Updated package.json and package-lock.json with the new dependency

### 2. Code Integration
Modified: `dashboard/src/App.jsx`
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/react'`
- Added `<Analytics />` component inside the App component's return statement
- The Analytics component is placed within the QueryClientProvider to ensure it has access to the entire application context

### 3. Framework-Specific Implementation
Following the official Vercel documentation for React/Vite projects:
- Used the React-specific package import path: `@vercel/analytics/react`
- Added the Analytics component at the root level of the application
- The component will automatically track page views and web vitals

### 4. Build Verification
- Successfully built the project with `npm run build`
- Build completed without errors in 1.11s
- Output size: 810.12 kB (232.00 kB gzipped)

## Implementation Notes

The Analytics component was added to the App component's JSX return statement, positioned after the Dashboard component but within the QueryClientProvider. This placement ensures:
- Analytics is initialized once when the app loads
- It can track all user interactions throughout the application
- It doesn't interfere with the existing application structure

## Next Steps for Deployment

To activate analytics tracking:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel using `vercel deploy`
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard for this project
3. Verify tracking by checking the browser Network tab for `/_vercel/insights/*` requests after deployment

The implementation follows the latest official Vercel documentation (retrieved from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart on May 20, 2026).
